    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born Isaïe Tasumbu Tawosa to a Tetela family in the Kasaï, Lumumba worked as a postal clerk and beer salesman before leading the Mouvement National Congolais from 1958, a party that did not draw on a particular ethnic base.</li><li>He became prime minister in June 1960, but within days an army mutiny, a Belgian intervention and the secession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a>, and President Kasa-Vubu dismissed him on 5 September.</li><li>Arrested by Mobutu's troops in December 1960 and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> on 17 January 1961, he has been remembered above all as a martyr, a status that historians such as David Van Reybrouck say owes more to his death than to his political successes.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 30 June 1960, Lumumba, who had not been scheduled to speak, answered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baudouin-of-belgium">King Baudouin</a>'s independence day speech with an impromptu address; Time magazine called it a &quot;vicious attack&quot;.</p></aside>
    </section>

